\title[Product constructions for snake-in-the-box codes]{Product constructions for snake-in-the-box codes:\\ half the multiplicative term, and a bulk obstruction to the rest}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{September 10, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
A \emph{snake} of length $L$ in the hypercube $Q_n$ is an induced path $v_0,\dots,v_L$ in the graph on $\{0,1\}^n$ whose edges join strings at Hamming distance $1$; the snake-in-the-box number $s(n)$ is the largest such $L$. Since $Q_{m+n}$ splits metrically as $Q_m\times Q_n$, it is natural to conjecture that snakes multiply: that there is an absolute constant $C$ such that snakes of lengths $L$ in $Q_m$ and $K$ in $Q_n$ combine into a snake of length at least $(L+1)(K+1)-C(L+K)$ in $Q_{m+n}$, the loss being confined to the interfaces between the factors.

We settle the status of this mechanism. Positively, the product of two snakes carries an \emph{induced} copy of the grid $P_{L+1}\gridprod P_{K+1}$ inside $Q_{m+n}$, and an explicit boustrophedon (``comb'') induced path of length $\comb(L,K)=\lfloor L/2\rfloor(K+2)+K$ yields $s(m)s(n)\le 2s(m+n)$ and $(s(m)+1)(s(n)+1)\le 2s(m+n)+s(n)+2$; a complementary L-shape of length $L+K$ gives $s(m)+s(n)\le s(m+n)$ and $s(n)+1\le s(n+1)$. Negatively, an induced path in a grid never contains all four cells of a $2\times 2$ square, so any snake supported on the product vertex set has at most $3\lceil (L+1)/2\rceil\lceil (K+1)/2\rceil\approx\frac34 (L+1)(K+1)$ vertices; consequently, for every constant $C$ and all $L,K\ge 8C+11$, no product-supported snake attains $(L+1)(K+1)-C(L+K)$. The loss of a product construction is $\Theta(LK)$, not $O(L+K)$: bounded chord repair inside the product grid is impossible. Finally $s(2)=2$ forces $C\ge 1$. We quantify the remaining gap---the comb attains a fraction $(K+2)/(2(K+1))$ of the grid, exactly $3/4$ when $K=1$---and conjecture that the true asymptotic density of a longest induced path in an $a\times b$ grid is $2/3$.
\end{abstract}

